+### Tailwind
+
+Veneer declares no Tailwind dependency and ships no rule whose reason is Tailwind. A consumer who
+already builds with Tailwind pairs the two by choosing a profile, which is the entry stylesheet
+their build compiles. Veneer supports Tailwind 4.3.
+
+The following table names each profile, what its entry declares, and where the pairing is proved.
+
+| Profile      | Entry                                                                       | Layers Tailwind fills in a consumer build | Tailwind parts                                          | Proof                                                                      |
+| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `standalone` | The cascade alone.                                                          | None.                                     | None.                                                   | [layer order and position independence](../tests/src/styles/index.test.ts) |
+| `tailwind`   | The order line, the composable imports, the source rules, then the cascade. | `theme` and `utilities`.                  | `tailwindcss/theme.css` and `tailwindcss/utilities.css` | [stylesheet profiles](../tests/tailwind/profiles.test.ts)                  |
+| `preflight`  | The order line, the bare import, the source rules, then the cascade.        | `theme`, `base`, and `utilities`.         | `tailwindcss`                                           | [stylesheet profiles](../tests/tailwind/profiles.test.ts)                  |
+
+Tailwind fills a layer only for the utilities it generates from the markup your `@source` rule
+names. The `utilities` layer carries those rules, the `theme` layer carries the variables those
+rules read and no others, and `base` carries preflight under the bare import. A build whose markup
+uses no Tailwind utility fills none of them. Where a generated utility registers a custom property
+of its own, Tailwind also generates a `properties` layer and declares `@layer properties;` ahead of
+the order line your entry wrote. That placement adds a layer in front of Veneer's and leaves
+Veneer's named layers in their declared order;
+[stylesheet profiles](../tests/tailwind/profiles.test.ts) reads both readings, the compiled
+stylesheet's own order and the order the document resolves against.
+
+The `standalone` profile is the product, and § Styles states it: import `@orkestrel/veneer/styles`
+and nothing else.
+
+In each recipe that follows, `@source './src'` is the line you change: it names your own markup
+directory, which is what Tailwind scans to decide which utilities to generate. The first recipe is
+the `tailwind` profile, which composes Tailwind's theme and utilities and leaves the reset to
+Veneer's own `reset` and `elements` layers.
+
+```css
+@layer theme, reset, base, elements, components, utilities;
+@import 'tailwindcss/theme.css' layer(theme);
+@import 'tailwindcss/utilities.css' layer(utilities);
+/* Your own markup directory. */
+@source './src';
+@source not inline("caption-bottom caption-top col-auto col-1 col-2 col-3 col-4 col-5 col-6 col-7 col-8 col-9 col-10 col-11 col-12 container table");
+@import '@orkestrel/veneer/styles';
+```
+
+The following recipe is the `preflight` profile, which takes the bare import so Tailwind's own reset
+fills the `base` layer. Veneer's `elements` layer sits after `base`, so the reset arrives under
+Veneer's element treatment rather than over it.
+
+```css
+@layer theme, reset, base, elements, components, utilities;
+@import 'tailwindcss';
+/* Your own markup directory. */
+@source './src';
+@source not inline("caption-bottom caption-top col-auto col-1 col-2 col-3 col-4 col-5 col-6 col-7 col-8 col-9 col-10 col-11 col-12 container table");
+@import '@orkestrel/veneer/styles';
+```
+
+No proof compiles either recipe in the shape it ships here. The executed consumer-shaped profile,
+with a markup fixture and the trailing cascade import, lands with the shared-name proof.
+
+The order line never changes between profiles. It is the line `src/styles/_tokens.scss` declares,
+and declaring it ahead of the Tailwind import is what makes Tailwind's own narrower order merge as
+a no-op.
+
+The exclusion line names the class names Veneer and Tailwind both declare and Veneer declares
+without `!important`. A shared name Veneer ships with its `!important` declaration wins by
+importance whatever the layer order, so it needs no entry; a shared name Veneer declares normally
+would otherwise resolve to Tailwind's rule, so the line withholds that name from Tailwind's
+generation and Veneer's declaration is the only one the page carries.
+
+`tests/setup.css` is where the workspace writes that line. The profiles proof reads it from that
+file rather than repeating it, and holds every written copy equal to it — the recipes in this
+section and `tests/fixtures/tailwind/preflight.css`; see
+[stylesheet profiles](../tests/tailwind/profiles.test.ts). So a release that ships another shared
+class name extends the line, and every copy left behind reddens until it matches.
+
+The workspace executes the profiles from `tests/setup.css` for the `tailwind` profile and
+`tests/fixtures/tailwind/preflight.css` for the `preflight` profile. Those profiles scan the
+candidate list the `configs/src/vite.tailwind.config.ts` wrapper derives from the built cascade
+rather than your markup, and every name that list offers is on the exclusion line, so the executed
+`tailwind` profile emits nothing at all — and that silence is what proves the exclusion complete.
+The instrument `tests/fixtures/tailwind/unexcluded.css` drops the line and adds an
+`@source inline(…)` control naming utilities the cascade declares no class for, so the same
+compilation emits something and the composed imports are read from what it emits. The wrapper
+attaches the `@tailwindcss/postcss` plugin, writes the candidate list before Tailwind compiles, and
+declares the `src:tailwind` project. Run `npm run test:src:tailwind`, which builds the cascade
+first.
+
